<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Neriglissar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 560–556 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Neriglissar was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> from 560 to 556 BCE. A leading official and son-in-law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a>, he took the throne after the murder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amel-marduk">Amel-Marduk</a>, which he probably organised. In 557 BCE he campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appuashu">Appuashu</a> of Pirindu. His young son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labashi-marduk">Labashi-Marduk</a> succeeded him but was overthrown within months by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Neriglissar, in Akkadian Nergal-šar-uṣur, meaning Nergal, protect the king, was the fourth king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Neo-Babylonian Empire</a>. He was unrelated to earlier Babylonian kings and may have been of Aramean ancestry. His own inscriptions call him the son of Bel-shum-ishkun, who was probably a man of the Aramean clan of the Puqudu. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> he was an active businessman and landowner, with estates at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sippar">Sippar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, one of the king's leading officials with the title Simmagir, and an army officer of the rank rab māg.</p><p>He married one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar</a>'s daughters, possibly Kashshaya, a proposal made by David B. Weisberg in 1974 that later historians have accepted. She may have been older than any of the king's sons, so Neriglissar may have stood for a wealthier and better established branch of the family, set against the younger and more legitimate branch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amel-marduk">Amel-Marduk</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The last document dated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amel-marduk">Amel-Marduk</a> is a contract of 7 August 560 BCE; four days later documents are dated to Neriglissar. The Babylonian writer Berossus says Neriglissar plotted against Amel-Marduk, who had ruled capriciously, and had him killed. To secure his position Neriglissar married his daughter Gigitum to Nabu-shuma-ukin, administrator of the Ezida temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borsippa">Borsippa</a>. He continued work on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esagila">Esagila</a>, the main temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, and repaired the royal palace and the bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>.</p><p>In his third year, 557 BCE, he marched into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> after rumours that Appuwashu, king of Pirindu, planned to raid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. Neriglissar defeated Appuwashu's ambush, pursued him through mountainous country to his capital Ura and sacked it, then destroyed the city of Kirshu. The contemporary chronicle says he took the island of Pitusu by boat and set fires along the passes towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a>. Appuwashu escaped, but Pirindu became a buffer state between the Babylonian, Lydian and Median realms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Neriglissar turned for home in February 556 BCE. The last documents of his reign date from 12 and 16 April 556 BCE, and the Uruk King List gives him three years and eight months. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labashi-marduk">Labashi-Marduk</a> succeeded him but was deposed and killed after two or three months in a coup, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a> took the throne. The reasons are unclear; perhaps the family was seen as commoners without noble blood. Neriglissar was nonetheless remembered favourably, and Nabonidus names him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar</a> as a good king.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Neriglissar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Four kings, Amel-Marduk, <b>Neriglissar</b>, Labashi-Marduk and Nabonidus, took the throne in the six years after Nebuchadnezzar's death in 562 BCE, and most of the successions involved assassination or coup.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Babylonian records call part of it Pirindu, and <b>Neriglissar</b> of the Chaldean Empire fought its king Appuashu in 557 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-cilicia">Kingdom of Cilicia</a></li></ul></section>
